page Portfolio

type Project {id, name, blurb, stack, area, year:number, live:bool}
data projects:Project[] GET /api/projects

type Message {id, name, email, body}
data messages:Message[] GET /api/messages
  send POST /api/messages {name,email,body} optimistic:append

state area=all|compilers|web|research
state name=""
state email=""
state body=""
state newsletter=false

nav Ravi Kishan
  link Work #work
  link About #about
  link Archive #archive
  link Contact #contact
  btn "Download CV" outline /cv.pdf

hero
  h1 I build compilers, and the interfaces that sit on top of them
  p Systems engineer working on developer tooling — language design, code generation, and the parts of a product people actually touch.
  btn "Get in touch" primary /contact
  link "Read the work" #work

section #work Selected work cols=3
  card "GUML" | An intermediate representation and compiler for LLM-generated interfaces. Rust, WebAssembly, 162 tests. Emits React that typechecks under strict mode.
  card "Zimyo platform" | HR platform serving payroll and attendance at scale. Owned the reporting pipeline and cut month-end close from hours to minutes.
  card "Ledger" | Double-entry accounting core with an append-only journal. Postgres, Rust, and property tests instead of hope.

section #about About
  h2 Twelve years of shipping things that other engineers depend on
  p I like the layer where correctness is checkable — parsers, compilers, schedulers, anything where a test can tell you the truth. Most of my work is in Rust and TypeScript, and most of it exists to make somebody else's job smaller.
  p Before tooling I spent six years on product teams, which is why I care whether the thing renders. A compiler that emits inaccessible markup has not finished its job.
  row between
    metric {projects.count}
    metric {projects.live.count}

section #stack How I work cols=3
  card "Measured, not asserted" | Every number in a README is traceable to a command you can run. If it is an estimate, it says so.
  card "Errors are a product surface" | Diagnostics get spans, stable codes, and a suggested fix. The best error message is one a machine can apply.
  card "Tests before trust" | Two bugs in the last scaffold were caught by tests written before the code was believed.

section #archive Everything else
  p Filter by area. The list, its loading skeleton, its empty state and its error banner are generated — none of it is written here.
  tabs area
  table projects where={area} sort=year
    text {name}
    text {stack}
    text {year}
    check {live} aria="Live"
    empty Nothing in this area yet.

section #contact Contact
  h2 Tell me what you are building
  p The fastest way to reach me is this form. I reply to everything that is not a recruiter template.
  form >messages.send{name:name,email:email,body:body}; name=""; email=""; body=""
    input name aria="Your name" placeholder="Your name" required
    input email kind=email aria="Email address" placeholder="you@example.com" required
    input body aria="What you are building" placeholder="A sentence or two" required
    toggle {newsletter} aria="Send me occasional notes about compilers"
    btn "Send message" primary disabled={!email.trim()} busy="Sending…"

faq open=1
  Are you available for contract work? | Yes, for short engagements on compilers, developer tooling or data-heavy back ends.
  What do you not take on? | Ad tech, and anything that needs a fresh design system built from nothing in under a month.
  Do you mentor? | Yes, one or two people at a time, free, usually engineers moving into systems work.

footer "© 2026 Ravi Kishan"
  link Email /contact
  link GitHub /github
  link LinkedIn /linkedin
